.TH CLOG_TRACE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_trace \- emit a log line at TRACE level
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_trace(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_TRACE level and picks up __FILE__, __LINE__
and __func__ for you.  It appends no backtrace.

The output format is:
  ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=TRACE proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>) src=<file>:<line> func=<fn> [fields] msg=<text>

When the minimum level of the logger is above CLOG_TRACE, the call does
nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
TRACE is the finest level and produces a lot of output, so turn it on
only when you diagnose low-level behaviour.
Every clog_<level> macro has the same thread-safety guarantee: the
shared write lock covers the whole cycle that formats and writes a
record, so a partial line never ends up between the lines of another
thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clog_trace(lg, "entered function, x=%d", x);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_debug (3),
.BR clog_info (3),
